bda }_{3}=2+2\\sqrt{13},$ what type of point is $a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186434751 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a2b77d8-21b3-512a-8f60-975fa8c9b29e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434751 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-6<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =2-2\\sqrt{13}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =2+2\\sqrt{13}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1045 "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186445202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 10